      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "I understand that the APA style can seem overwhelming. Regarding your question, with APA 7th style, for a work with five authors, you indeed list all their names in the reference list. For the in-text citation, the full list of authors is used for the first citation, followed by the journal abbreviation and year (e.g., Smith et al., 2020). After listing the first set of authors in full and providing enough information for a reader to identify the work (the full names of the first two authors, year, journal, volume, and pages; or if these are not available, a short abbreviation of the title of the article), you can then use the conjunction 'et al.' after the next in-text citation (Smith et al., 2020, p. 15).\n\nHere's how it would look in practice:\n\n1st citation: in-text (Smith et al., 2020)\nSubsequent citations: in-text (<|Smith et al., 2020|>, 2020, p. 15)\n\nThe rules do become more expansive with the inclusion of various contributors and working groups, but they intend to provide an exact and clear reference for the reader, which can facilitate quicker identification of resources later",
